# 20 hard English practice passages (evergreen topics), written for Alert Typing.
EN = [
("Fiscal Federalism in India", "Fiscal federalism concerns the division of taxing powers and spending responsibilities between the Union and the States. The Constitution assigns the more elastic sources of revenue - income tax, corporation tax and customs duties - to the Centre, whereas States bear the larger share of expenditure on health, education, policing and agriculture. To correct this 'vertical imbalance', Article 280 provides for a Finance Commission, constituted every five years, which recommends how the divisible pool of central taxes should be shared. The Goods and Services Tax, introduced on 1 July 2017, further transformed the system: a large number of indirect taxes were subsumed, and decisions are now taken by the GST Council, chaired by the Union Finance Minister, in which the States collectively hold two-thirds of the voting weight. Critics argue that the growing use of cesses and surcharges, which are not shared with the States, dilutes their rightful share. Nevertheless, cooperative federalism, built on negotiation rather than confrontation, remains the most practical path for a country as diverse as India."),
("The Doctrine of Basic Structure", "In Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973), a thirteen-judge Bench of the Supreme Court held, by a narrow majority of seven to six, that although Parliament may amend any provision of the Constitution under Article 368, it cannot alter its 'basic structure'. The Court did not offer an exhaustive list; instead, subsequent judgments identified features such as the supremacy of the Constitution, the rule of law, judicial review, secularism, federalism, free and fair elections and the separation of powers. In Minerva Mills (1980), the Court struck down clauses of the Forty-second Amendment that had sought to make constitutional amendments immune from judicial scrutiny. Supporters regard the doctrine as an essential safeguard against the misuse of a temporary majority; critics contend that it allows unelected judges to substitute their own views for those of the people's representatives. Whatever the debate, the doctrine has become a defining feature of Indian constitutionalism and has been cited by courts in Bangladesh, Pakistan and several other countries."),
("Inflation Targeting and the Repo Rate", "Since 2016, India has followed a framework of flexible inflation targeting. Under the amended Reserve Bank of India Act, the government, in consultation with the RBI, fixes a target of 4 per cent for consumer price inflation, with a tolerance band of 2 to 6 per cent. A six-member Monetary Policy Committee - three members from the RBI, including the Governor, and three external experts - meets at least four times a year and decides the policy repo rate by majority vote; the Governor has a casting vote in case of a tie. The repo rate is the interest rate at which the central bank lends short-term funds to commercial banks against government securities. When inflation rises above the target, the Committee may increase the rate, making loans costlier and cooling demand; when growth slows, it may reduce the rate to encourage borrowing and investment. If inflation remains outside the band for three consecutive quarters, the RBI must submit a written report to the government explaining the reasons and the corrective steps it proposes."),
("Panchayati Raj after the 73rd Amendment", "The Constitution (Seventy-third Amendment) Act, 1992, which came into force on 24 April 1993, gave constitutional status to rural local self-government. It created Part IX of the Constitution and added the Eleventh Schedule, listing 29 subjects - including agriculture, minor irrigation, drinking water, primary education and rural housing - that States may devolve to Panchayats. The Act provides for a three-tier structure at the village, intermediate and district levels; regular elections every five years conducted by a State Election Commission; reservation of seats for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes in proportion to their population; and reservation of not less than one-third of the seats for women, which many States, including Bihar and Uttarakhand, have raised to one-half. A State Finance Commission reviews the financial position of Panchayats every five years. Despite these provisions, experts point out that genuine decentralisation depends on the 'three Fs' - funds, functions and functionaries - and that several States have transferred responsibilities without transferring adequate money or trained staff."),
("Groundwater Depletion in North India", "India extracts more groundwater than any other country, and the problem is most acute in north-western States such as Punjab, Haryana and Rajasthan, as well as in parts of western Uttar Pradesh. Satellite measurements by the GRACE mission indicated a steady decline in water storage across the region during the 2000s. The causes are interlinked: free or heavily subsidised electricity for farm pumps, assured procurement of water-intensive crops such as paddy and sugarcane, and the near absence of any charge for the water itself. As the water table falls, farmers must install deeper and more powerful pumps, raising costs and pushing smallholders into debt; in some districts, groundwater also contains excessive fluoride, arsenic or nitrate. Remedies include the Atal Bhujal Yojana, which encourages community-led management of aquifers, the promotion of micro-irrigation, crop diversification towards pulses and millets, and the revival of traditional ponds and step-wells. Experts emphasise, however, that such measures succeed only when farmers receive reliable incentives to change long-established cropping patterns."),
("The Right to Information Act, 2005", "The Right to Information Act, 2005, empowers every citizen to seek information held by public authorities, including ministries, municipal bodies, public sector undertakings and organisations substantially financed by the government. An application, accompanied by a nominal fee of ten rupees, is submitted to the Public Information Officer, who must reply within thirty days; where the information concerns the life or liberty of a person, the reply must be given within forty-eight hours. Applicants from families below the poverty line are exempt from paying the fee. If the reply is unsatisfactory, the applicant may file a first appeal within the department and, thereafter, a second appeal before the Central or State Information Commission. Section 8 lists exemptions, such as information affecting national security, cabinet papers before a decision is taken, and personal information unrelated to public activity. The Act has exposed irregularities in ration distribution, road construction and examinations; however, activists have expressed concern over pending appeals, vacancies in Information Commissions and the attacks faced by some applicants."),
("Food Security and the Public Distribution System", "The National Food Security Act, 2013, gives a legal entitlement to subsidised food grains to up to 75 per cent of the rural population and 50 per cent of the urban population. Priority households receive five kilograms of grain per person per month, while families covered under the Antyodaya Anna Yojana, the poorest of the poor, receive thirty-five kilograms per household. The Act also provides nutritional support to pregnant women, lactating mothers and children through anganwadis and the mid-day meal scheme, and it designates the eldest woman of the household, aged eighteen or above, as its head for the purpose of issuing ration cards. The Public Distribution System has been strengthened through digitisation of ration cards, Aadhaar-based authentication at fair price shops and the One Nation One Ration Card scheme, which allows migrant workers to draw their entitlement anywhere in the country. Challenges remain, including errors in biometric authentication, leakage during transport and the need to diversify rations with pulses, edible oil and millets to address hidden hunger."),
("Climate Change and Indian Agriculture", "Agriculture supports nearly half of India's workforce, and it is exceptionally vulnerable to climate change. Rising temperatures shorten the grain-filling period of wheat; erratic monsoon rainfall, with long dry spells followed by intense downpours, damages kharif crops; and an increase in extreme events such as heatwaves, cyclones and hailstorms destroys standing crops within hours. Studies by the Indian Council of Agricultural Research suggest that, in the absence of adaptation, yields of rice and wheat in several regions could decline significantly by the end of the century. Adaptation therefore requires a combination of measures: climate-resilient seed varieties that tolerate heat, drought or flooding; efficient irrigation; weather-based crop insurance under the Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana; agro-advisories delivered through mobile phones; and the restoration of soil organic carbon. At the same time, agriculture itself emits greenhouse gases, notably methane from flooded paddy fields and livestock, and nitrous oxide from fertilisers. Practices such as direct-seeded rice, alternate wetting and drying and balanced fertiliser use can reduce these emissions while saving water and money."),
("Judicial Independence and the Collegium", "The independence of the judiciary is widely regarded as a basic feature of the Indian Constitution. Judges of the Supreme Court and the High Courts are appointed by the President; however, through the 'Three Judges Cases' of 1981, 1993 and 1998, the Supreme Court developed the collegium system, under which the Chief Justice of India and the four senior-most judges recommend names for appointment to the Supreme Court. In 2014, Parliament enacted the National Judicial Appointments Commission Act, which proposed a six-member body including the Law Minister and two eminent persons. In October 2015, a Constitution Bench struck down the law by a majority of four to one, holding that it compromised the primacy of the judiciary in appointments. Supporters of the collegium argue that it shields appointments from political pressure; critics point to the absence of transparent criteria, the lack of reasons for decisions and the delays in filling vacancies. A revised Memorandum of Procedure has been under discussion for years, reflecting the difficulty of balancing independence with accountability."),
("Renewable Energy Transition", "India has set an ambitious target of achieving 500 gigawatts of non-fossil electricity capacity by 2030 and net-zero emissions by 2070, commitments announced at the Glasgow climate conference in 2021. Solar energy has led this transition: large solar parks in Rajasthan, Gujarat and Karnataka, rooftop installations under the PM Surya Ghar scheme and solar pumps for farmers under PM-KUSUM have all expanded capacity rapidly. Wind energy, concentrated along the western and southern coasts, complements solar generation because winds are often stronger during the monsoon and at night. However, the variability of renewable power creates new challenges for grid managers. Electricity must be balanced second by second, which requires battery storage, pumped hydro projects, flexible operation of thermal plants and strong inter-State transmission lines, including the Green Energy Corridors. Land acquisition, the import dependence of solar cells and the recycling of old panels and batteries also require careful policy attention. Nevertheless, falling costs have made renewable energy one of the cheapest sources of new electricity in the country."),
("Urbanisation and the Smart Cities Mission", "According to the 2011 Census, about 31 per cent of India's population lived in urban areas, and this share is projected to rise steadily in the coming decades. Cities generate a disproportionate share of national income, yet many suffer from congestion, inadequate housing, waterlogging, poor waste management and polluted air. The Smart Cities Mission, launched in June 2015, selected one hundred cities through a competition and supported projects such as integrated command-and-control centres, smart roads, solid waste management and the redevelopment of selected areas. Alongside it, the Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation focused on water supply, sewerage and green spaces, while the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana - Urban aimed to provide affordable housing. Evaluations have noted both achievements and limitations: technology-driven projects improved certain services, but many benefits were concentrated in small pockets, and municipal bodies often lacked the financial and administrative capacity to sustain them. Urban experts argue that empowered elected mayors, stronger municipal finances through property tax reform, and integrated planning of land use and transport are essential for liveable cities."),
("Disaster Management Framework", "India is exposed to a wide range of hazards: nearly 59 per cent of its land mass is prone to moderate or severe earthquakes, around 12 per cent to floods, and its long coastline to cyclones and tsunamis. The Disaster Management Act, 2005, shifted the national approach from relief-centred response to a holistic cycle of prevention, mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery. It established the National Disaster Management Authority, chaired by the Prime Minister, along with State and District Disaster Management Authorities, and created the National Disaster Response Force, a specialised force trained for search and rescue. The success of this framework is visible in the evacuation of lakhs of people before cyclones on the Odisha coast, which has reduced deaths dramatically compared with the super cyclone of 1999. Nevertheless, gaps remain in urban flood management, enforcement of building codes, early warning for flash floods and landslides in the Himalayas, and protection for the poorest communities who live in the most vulnerable areas. The Sendai Framework, adopted in 2015, emphasises investment in risk reduction rather than spending only after a disaster."),
("Ethics in Public Administration", "Public servants exercise considerable discretion: they grant licences, award contracts, select beneficiaries and enforce laws. Ethics in administration, therefore, is not an abstract ideal but a practical necessity. The Second Administrative Reforms Commission, in its report on ethics in governance, recommended a code of ethics for ministers, legislators and civil servants, the protection of whistle-blowers and the simplification of procedures that create opportunities for corruption. The foundational values of the civil services include integrity, impartiality, objectivity, dedication to public service, empathy towards weaker sections and tolerance of diverse views. Conflicts of interest arise when a personal relationship or financial interest may influence an official decision; the appropriate response is disclosure and recusal. Technology has reduced some opportunities for misconduct - online applications, direct benefit transfers and e-procurement limit discretion - yet ethical judgement remains indispensable when rules are silent or contradictory. As the Nolan Committee in the United Kingdom observed, holders of public office should act solely in terms of the public interest and should be as open as possible about their decisions."),
("Cyber Security and Digital Payments", "India has become one of the world's largest markets for digital payments, led by the Unified Payments Interface, which processes billions of transactions every month. This growth has, however, attracted cyber criminals who exploit weak passwords, fake customer-care numbers, phishing links and remote-access applications to steal money. The Information Technology Act, 2000, along with its rules, provides the legal framework for electronic records, digital signatures and cyber offences, while the Indian Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT-In) acts as the national agency for responding to incidents. Citizens can report financial fraud on the national helpline 1930 or on the cyber crime reporting portal; prompt reporting within the 'golden hour' greatly increases the chance of freezing stolen funds. Banks and payment providers are required to adopt two-factor authentication, transaction limits and fraud-monitoring systems. Nonetheless, awareness remains the strongest defence. Users should never share an OTP or PIN, should verify the identity of callers, should download applications only from official stores and should be wary of offers that appear too good to be true."),
("Women's Self-Help Groups and Rural Livelihoods", "Self-help groups, usually consisting of ten to twenty women from similar economic backgrounds, have transformed rural livelihoods across India. Members save small amounts regularly, lend to one another for urgent needs and, after building a record of discipline, gain access to bank credit through the SHG-Bank Linkage Programme pioneered by NABARD in 1992. The Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana - National Rural Livelihoods Mission, launched in 2011, has mobilised crores of women into such groups and organised them into village organisations and cluster-level federations. Beyond credit, these groups run small enterprises in dairy, poultry, tailoring, food processing and handicrafts; some members serve as banking correspondents or community resource persons. Studies suggest that participation increases women's confidence, mobility and role in household decisions, and strengthens their voice in Gram Sabhas. Challenges persist: many enterprises remain small and lack access to markets, technology and branding, and repayment can become a burden when incomes fail. Linking groups with e-commerce platforms, cold chains and skill training can help convert subsistence activities into sustainable businesses."),
("The Role of the Comptroller and Auditor General", "The Comptroller and Auditor General of India, appointed by the President under Article 148, audits the accounts of the Union, the States and bodies substantially financed by government. To protect independence, the CAG holds office for six years or until the age of sixty-five, whichever is earlier, can be removed only in the manner of a Supreme Court judge, and is ineligible for further office under the government after retirement. The CAG's reports on Union accounts are submitted to the President, who causes them to be laid before Parliament; they are then examined by the Public Accounts Committee, traditionally chaired by a member of the opposition. Over the years, audit has evolved from checking the legality of expenditure to 'performance audit', which asks whether public money achieved its intended outcomes economically, efficiently and effectively. Reports on telecom spectrum, coal block allocation and defence procurement generated intense public debate. Critics sometimes question the methods used to estimate presumptive losses; nevertheless, an independent audit institution remains central to holding the executive accountable to the legislature and, ultimately, to citizens."),
("Biodiversity and Protected Areas", "India occupies only about 2.4 per cent of the world's land area, yet it harbours roughly 7 to 8 per cent of recorded species, and it contains four of the world's thirty-six biodiversity hotspots: the Himalaya, the Western Ghats, the Indo-Burma region and Sundaland. The Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972, provides for national parks, wildlife sanctuaries, conservation reserves and community reserves, while the Biological Diversity Act, 2002, regulates access to biological resources and seeks fair sharing of benefits with local communities. Project Tiger, launched in 1973, and the subsequent creation of the National Tiger Conservation Authority helped the tiger population recover substantially, according to successive all-India estimates. Conservation, however, faces complex trade-offs. Highways and railway lines fragment forest corridors, mining and dams submerge habitats, and human-wildlife conflict causes loss of lives and crops near protected areas. The Forest Rights Act, 2006, recognises the rights of forest-dwelling communities, and experience shows that conservation succeeds best when local people share its benefits rather than bearing only its costs."),
("Financial Inclusion and Jan Dhan Accounts", "Financial inclusion means providing affordable access to savings accounts, credit, insurance and pensions for all sections of society, especially low-income households. The Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana, launched in August 2014, opened crores of zero-balance accounts within a short period, offering a RuPay debit card with accident insurance and, for eligible holders, an overdraft facility. Combined with Aadhaar and mobile phones - the so-called JAM trinity - these accounts enabled the government to transfer subsidies and welfare payments directly to beneficiaries, reducing leakages caused by intermediaries. Social security schemes such as Pradhan Mantri Jeevan Jyoti Bima Yojana, Pradhan Mantri Suraksha Bima Yojana and Atal Pension Yojana extended low-cost insurance and pension coverage. Nevertheless, opening an account is only the first step. A significant share of accounts have seen limited activity, and rural customers continue to face difficulties such as distant branches, network failures and limited financial literacy. Business correspondents, payment banks and digital literacy campaigns therefore play a vital role in turning access into meaningful use."),
("The Election Commission and Free Elections", "Article 324 of the Constitution vests the superintendence, direction and control of elections to Parliament, State legislatures and the offices of President and Vice-President in the Election Commission of India. Originally a single-member body, the Commission has consisted of a Chief Election Commissioner and two Election Commissioners since 1993, with decisions taken by majority. The Model Code of Conduct, which takes effect from the announcement of the election schedule, restricts the announcement of new schemes, the misuse of official machinery and appeals to caste or religious sentiments. Electronic Voting Machines, used across the country since 2004, are now paired with the Voter Verifiable Paper Audit Trail, which allows a voter to see a printed slip of the vote cast for a few seconds. Electoral reforms remain under discussion, including the decriminalisation of politics, limits on expenditure by parties, the regulation of political advertisements on social media and the idea of simultaneous elections. Ultimately, the credibility of elections rests on the Commission's impartiality, the vigilance of political parties and the participation of informed voters."),
("Skill Development and the Demographic Dividend", "With a median age below thirty, India possesses one of the youngest populations among major economies; this 'demographic dividend', however, will translate into prosperity only if young people acquire skills that match the needs of employers. Surveys have repeatedly shown that a large proportion of graduates are not considered job-ready, while industries report shortages of welders, electricians, nurses, technicians and software professionals. The Skill India Mission, the Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana, Industrial Training Institutes and the National Apprenticeship Promotion Scheme aim to bridge this gap. The National Education Policy, 2020, also seeks to integrate vocational education into schools and colleges, so that students can learn trades alongside academic subjects and earn credits for both. Experts recommend closer partnership with industry in designing courses, recognition of prior learning for informal workers, apprenticeships with fair stipends and better information about jobs in emerging sectors such as renewable energy, electric vehicles and logistics. Equally important is changing social attitudes, so that skilled manual work is respected as a dignified and rewarding career."),
]
